# Backend learnings (packages/backend)

Contextual advice for future tasks in the Convex backend. Reference before working on
partner-API, webhook, trigger, or convex-test tasks.

## Partner API / webhooks (added 2026-07-17)

- **Single choke-point for table-change side effects**: `convex/functions.ts` wraps every
  mutation with a convex-helpers `Triggers` registry. To react to ALL writes on a table
  (regardless of which mutation performs them), register a trigger there — do not wire
  per-mutation calls. Partner webhook emission lives in `convex/lib/webhookEmission.ts`
  and is registered for `rsvps` and `events`.
- **Trigger ordering matters**: triggers fire immediately at each `ctx.db` write. If a
  trigger reads sibling rows (e.g. webhook identity resolution reads `guestContacts`),
  those rows must be written BEFORE the row that fires the trigger. This is why
  `submitGuestRequest` upserts `guestContacts` before inserting the RSVP.
- **Guest identity**: synthetic clerk ids (`guest:<phoneHash>`) helpers live in
  `convex/lib/guestIdentity.ts` (extracted from rsvps.ts). Guest plaintext phone is
  durably stored only in `guestContacts` (by design; handoff rows expire).
- **API keys**: stored SHA-256-hashed (`lib/apiKeys.ts` reuses `hashOpaqueValue`).
  Webhook endpoint secrets are stored readable (AES needs key recovery) — an accepted,
  documented tradeoff (docs/partner-api.md).
- **HTTP surface**: `/api/v1/*` httpActions live in `convex/apiV1.ts` (auth middleware
  in `lib/apiRequestAuth.ts`), backed by internal functions in `convex/apiV1Data.ts`.
  Deliberately no CORS. `API_VERSION` and webhook event types come from
  `@coucou/sdk/api-v1` — single source shared with consumers; keep the sdk consumer
  helpers byte-compatible with `convex/lib/webhookCrypto.ts` (roundtrip-tested in
  `packages/sdk/__tests__/webhook-consumer.test.ts`).

## Partner erasure and account deletion (added 2026-10-27, anonymize since 2026-11-02, review fixes 2026-11-03, bounded batches 2026-11-04)

Use when deleting a person's data, or when a cleanup must not let triggers rebuild rows.

- Erasure anonymizes, it never deletes the RSVP. `anonymizeRsvp` in
  `convex/lib/rsvpAnonymization.ts` is the one routine: it strips every identity field
  (`REMOVED_IDENTITY_FIELDS`), gives the row a per-row `clerkUserId` (`anonymized-<id>`, or
  `guest:anonymized-<id>` so `isGuest` survives), sets `anonymizedAt`, re-keys the redemption
  and approvals and gives the ticket a fresh code, and deletes the side records and webhook
  deliveries. The event, list, statuses, party size, ticket status and check-in stay: no count
  a host or partner sees may change, so `rsvpAggregate` (keyed on event, status, list) needs no
  update and anonymization must never touch `ticketStatus` or `disabledAt`. A new identity field
  on `rsvps` must go into `REMOVED_IDENTITY_FIELDS`.
- Copies of the person live off the row under the RSVP id and every id a merge retired
  (`rsvpIdentityAliases.by_canonical`): webhook deliveries (deleted), audit entries with a JSON
  copy of the row (`auditLog.by_target`, metadata replaced by `redacted: "rsvp_anonymized"`, a
  person actor cleared; the person is every identity in `RsvpAnonymizationPerson.clerkUserIds`,
  merged accounts included), and SMS logs (`textBlastRecipients.sourceRsvpIds` found by phone
  hash, reply attempts and inbound receipts by their rsvp-id indexes; the id is removed, the log
  stays). A new table or audit writer that stores an RSVP id next to identity must be added to
  `anonymizeRsvp`.
- Cost: every read in `anonymizeRsvp` is keyed by the RSVP (profile values use
  `by_source_rsvp`), never by the person, so it does not grow with the person's history. Rows
  keyed by the person's phone (`textBlastRecipients`) are scanned once per transaction by
  `anonymizeRsvps` against all the ids it anonymized, 500 rows at most from a cursor. Keep any new
  person-keyed scan out of the per-RSVP routine and page it the same way. To prove a bound, wrap
  `ctx.db` and count documents read (`countDocumentsRead` in `api-v1-erasure.test.ts`).
- `buildRsvpTicketSnapshot` returns null for an anonymized RSVP, so partner GETs and webhooks
  never carry the new code. Host readers (`rsvps.listForEvent`, `redemptions.listForEvent`,
  exports) still show it: the code was never sent to anyone, and the host owns the ticket.
- Repair and backfill paths that write identity onto an existing row must skip `anonymizedAt`
  rows (`repairTextRsvpAssociations`, `backfillSmsConsentForEvent`).
- Size: `MAX_RSVPS_ANONYMIZED_PER_TRANSACTION` (50). Partner erasure anonymizes at most that
  many per request and answers `status: "partial"` so the partner repeats it. Account deletion
  anonymizes that many inline. The rest, and SMS recipient rows past one page, go to
  `rsvpAnonymizationBatches` rows (an `RsvpAnonymizationWork`: RSVP ids, identities, phone
  hashes, SMS-log ids and cursor; deleted when done) run by `rsvpAnonymizationBatches.run`,
  which reschedules itself until the work is done. The user row can go first because a batch
  reads nothing the deletion removes. The `crons.ts` sweep (`rerunStalled`, every 10 minutes)
  reruns a batch whose run was scheduled over 10 minutes ago and counts `attempts`; a high count
  in the logs is a batch that keeps failing.
- Pending rows: until its batch runs, a deleted account's RSVP still carries its phone.
  `pendingRsvpAnonymizations` marks it. A write that finds an existing RSVP by phone and would
  reuse it calls `finishPendingAnonymization` first (guest form, shared submission service,
  partner upsert, text-blast reply), which anonymizes the row so the write creates a new one. A
  guest claim skips pending rows (`isPendingAnonymization`). A new write path that finds an RSVP
  by phone or guest id must do the same.
- Callers: `POST /api/v1/erasures` (`convex/partnerErasure.ts`, the calling key's RSVPs only)
  and Coucou account deletion (`lib/deleteUserData.ts`, every RSVP matched by the account, its
  aliases and its phone).
- The erasure notice is `rsvp.updated` with null identity and `changes.reason: "erasure"`.
  `enqueueRsvpWebhookDeliveries` emits it for any update that sets `anonymizedAt`
  (`isRsvpAnonymization`). Account deletion is triggered, so the rsvps trigger sends it.
  `partnerErasure.ts` is an untriggered internal mutation, a deliberate exception to the
  trigger choke-point: the contact-sync triggers (`workspaceGuestProfiles` → `syncProfile` →
  `ensureContact`) would recreate the contact rows it deletes. It passes the change to
  `enqueueRsvpWebhookDeliveries` itself. `anonymizeRsvp` deletes stored deliveries before the
  patch, so the notice is the only delivery left.
- Anonymized rows match no account, phone or merge lookup, so later writes never re-attach a
  person: the partner upsert and the guest form create a new row. Readers that build people
  from RSVPs skip `anonymizedAt` rows (`syncContactRsvp`, the guest directory); a new one must
  too.
- Attribution is `rsvps.createdByApiClientId`, set only on the partner insert.
  `rsvps.apiClientId` is the last API writer and every partner write overwrites it, so never
  key ownership on it. Rows without a creator (inserted before this field) need the `rsvp.created`
  webhook delivery's `origin.apiClientId`, a non-text/form source and no absorbed merge.
  Identity merges keep the creator only when both rows share it
  (`mergedRsvpCreatorApiClientId`). Apply the same rule to any new merge path.
- A row the person submitted or edited through Coucou (form, signed-in app, text) is never
  anonymized by a partner: every such patch of an existing row sets `rsvps.personSubmittedAt`,
  and merges keep it when either row has it (`mergedRsvpPersonSubmittedAt`). A new
  person-facing write path that patches an RSVP must set it too.
  `rsvpIdentityAliases.retiredApiClientIds` lets the erasure report a merged row to the keys
  that wrote the absorbed one.
- Resolve caller-supplied RSVP ids with `resolveCanonicalRsvpId`: merges retire ids.
- `webhookDeliveries.payloadJson` keeps plaintext phone and name forever and hosts can
  re-send it, so any deletion path must also delete deliveries (`by_rsvp` index).
- A new top-level Convex module needs its two lines in the checked-in
  `convex/_generated/api.d.ts` (`convex codegen` needs a deployment).
- `bun run build` fails locally on Clerk prerender without env. Export the placeholder
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values from `.github/workflows/ci.yml` to reproduce CI.

## convex-test gotchas (bun test)

- **Scheduled function leaks**: mutations that `ctx.scheduler.runAfter(0, ...)` leave real
  timers that can fire after a test file ends → "Write outside of transaction" /
  "test began while previous transaction was still open" in LATER files. Either
  (a) `vi.useFakeTimers()` in beforeEach + `testBackend.finishAllScheduledFunctions(vi.runAllTimers)`
  after each mutation (see webhook-pipeline.test.ts, text-blasts.test.ts), or
  (b) track backends and drain in afterEach (see rsvp-guest-handoff.test.ts).
  Never switch to fake timers AFTER a real 0-delay timer is already queued — that races.
- **Parallel component calls fail**: `Promise.all` over aggregate `count()` calls inside
  one query throws "Bad queryId" under convex-test. Run component sub-queries
  sequentially.
- **httpAction tests**: `testBackend.fetch("/api/v1/...", init)` works; module map must
  include `http.ts` and every module reachable from the routed handlers.
- **Aggregate component**: register with `testBackend.registerComponent("rsvpAggregate",
  schema, modules)` importing from `node_modules/@convex-dev/aggregate/dist/esm/component/*`
  (copy the block from an existing test).
- **WebCrypto in mutations**: `crypto.subtle.digest` and `crypto.getRandomValues` work in
  the Convex default runtime and in convex-test — hashing/keygen inside mutations is fine.

## TypeScript

- Under TS 5.7+ libs, annotate WebCrypto-bound byte helpers to return
  ArrayBuffer-backed arrays (`new Uint8Array(new ArrayBuffer(n))`, inferred return type)
  or `crypto.subtle` calls fail on `Uint8Array<ArrayBufferLike>` vs `BufferSource`.
